		<title>We forgot a Pull Request when Splitting a Repo</title>
		<link>https://cromwellhaus.com/2016/08/we-forgot-a-pull-request-when-splitting-a-repo/</link>
		<comments>https://cromwellhaus.com/2016/08/we-forgot-a-pull-request-when-splitting-a-repo/#respond</comments>
		<pubDate>Thu, 01 Sep 2016 03:30:25 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[cromwellryan]]></dc:creator>
				<category><![CDATA[Uncategorized]]></category>

		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://cromwellhaus.com/?p=1548</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[We&#8217;re working on a little skunk works project at the office and it involved splitting a Github repository in two. After a successful split, I noticed we&#8217;d missed a PR that now impacts both repositories. My head went to git format-patch &#60;sha&#62;, but I didn&#8217;t recall an option to target a file spec and at first [&#8230;]]]></description>
				<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>We&#8217;re working on a little skunk works project at the office and it involved splitting a Github repository in two. After a successful split, I noticed we&#8217;d missed a PR that now impacts both repositories.</p>
<p>My head went to <code>git format-patch &lt;sha&gt;</code>, but I didn&#8217;t recall an option to target a file spec and at first glance it generates a patch for every commit after that SHA.</p>
<p><code>git show</code> on the other hand definitely advertises an option to filter<code>git show &lt;sha&gt; -- path/that/moved</code></p>
<p>Format that as <code>--pretty=email</code> and you can direct the output to a patch for</p>
<pre>git show --pretty=email &lt;sha&gt; -- path/that/moved&gt; pr-for-other-repo.patch
</pre>
<p>Now we can pull that into our other repo with <code> git am -3 pr-for-other-repo.patch</code>.</p>
<p>Turns out that <code>-- path/that/moved</code> semantic does work with <code>git format-patch</code>. So in the end, we could have just used</p>
<pre>git format-patch -1 -- path/that/moved
git am -3 partial-pr.patch
</pre>
]]></content:encoded>
